#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128958 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129142 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dce1c69-c79d-541a-b479-8fc127352c2a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "e6a96fc8-6ce5-5f47-8c20-709754ed7bc9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5811" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the graph of $y =2x^3- 6 x^2 +8?$\n\n![](images/3e/3e01f41691c4f157964157f30678f98977ac8bdf9908d89bd3e9a8817a6e2b91.png)" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the given polynomial:\n\n$$\nP(x)=2x^3 -6 x^2 + 8\n$$\n\nThe degree ($n=3$) is odd and the leading coefficient ($a_3 = 2$) is positive. Therefore, the polynomial has the following end behavior:\n\n- $P(x)$ approaches $-\\infty$ as as $x$ approaches $-\\infty.$\n\n- $P(x)$ approaches $\\infty$ as $x$ approaches $\\infty.$\n\nOnly the graph $\\textbf{C}$ satisfies these conditions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128961 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129144 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7cf112-ab08-5e7a-855d-91bb69ec7f76" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polynomial Given its Graph"}] :question/id #uuid "7098a9a5-23ea-5496-a492-191c9c1a0690" :question/math-academy-id "e-5815" :question/problem "![](images/84/84c37b1692f6807873d924fad0d3591298c2b46b780797ed258dcf32d2ff0d8a.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials is shown in the diagram above?\n\nI. $-2 x^4 + 6 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 6 x + 4$\n\nII. $-2 x^5 + 6 x^4 + 5 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 3 x + 1$\n\nIII. $3 x^4 + 5 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 3 x - 4$\n\nIV. $- x^4 + 5 x^3 - 2 x^2 - 3 x - 1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to-\\infty,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty.$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\nSo, $P(x)$ is neither II nor III.\n\nLastly, note that the $y$-intercept is $4,$ which means that $P(0)$ must be equal to $4.$\n\nTherefore, $P(x)$ is given by polynomial I."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186128962 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34aa8f4e-20f1-5dda-ac53-6eb411543897" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/id #uuid "fdba6918-c9be-5a77-b79c-4a5cf39bf6e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-5817" :question/problem "The graph of a polynomial $Q(x)$ is shown below.\n\n![](images/c6/c6729ee13a704ce630586cd4cbdde5c67e55c03f65f1840b2ca1f2b6c38186f2.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The coefficient of the leading term is positive.\n\nII. The degree of $Q(x)$ is odd.\n\nIII. The constant term is negative." :question/worked-solution "The end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $Q(x)\\to\\infty$ as $x\\to-\\infty,$ and\n\n- $Q(x)\\to-\\infty$ as $x\\to\\infty.$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $Q(x)$ is odd, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $Q(x)$ is negative.\n\nSo I is false, and II is true.\n\nLastly, note that the $y$-intercept is $0,$ which means that $Q(0)$ must be equal to $0,$ and therefore the constant term must be equal to $0.$ So, III is false.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561002 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129142 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dce1c69-c79d-541a-b479-8fc127352c2a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2fd81dde-f868-544e-aff5-0c8968413d49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None of the given options is correct" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560470} {:answer/id #uuid "5c2001ae-1d7f-585d-853b-10eb5b05ba35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560561} {:answer/id #uuid "6378b4e6-d24a-5390-ac0b-8fc40905840b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560574} {:answer/id #uuid "c888ed3f-9cc2-510e-b0ae-77b332919a39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560764} {:answer/id #uuid "cd12291a-e3f9-5963-8bb6-a811725792d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "D" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c2001ae-1d7f-585d-853b-10eb5b05ba35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560561} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc1b9076-e7a0-59dc-af73-fb509c4f74ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560951}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "60eb3184-1363-5380-874f-89b878456387" :question/math-academy-id "q-112077" :question/problem "![](images/ca/cac1263052200f4794e983bd11236175646d4c808b5f8fee81ccfb58de12c5cf.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be the graph of $y=4{x}^{4}+x-1?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the given polynomial:\n\n$$\nP(x)=4{x}^{4}+x-1\n$$\n\nThe degree $(n=4)$ is even and the leading coefficient $({a}_{4}=4)$ is positive. Therefore, the polynomial has the following end behavior:\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty .$\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nOnly the graph $B$ satisfies these conditions."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129144 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ef7cf112-ab08-5e7a-855d-91bb69ec7f76" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Polynomial Given its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "044e357f-937c-5bd9-add1-561e96b796b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=7{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560369} {:answer/id #uuid "14630fb7-9545-5c1e-8187-195636207c60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{2}+2x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560406} {:answer/id #uuid "1dfeaa55-d693-571f-970a-52c394bd4b98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2{x}^{6}+5{x}^{5}-x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560432} {:answer/id #uuid "5eb962d6-ab29-5e03-b451-b28b26aec307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560566} {:answer/id #uuid "ae6ea9d0-4acf-5f43-bd32-b0b64eb348e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-{x}^{3}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5eb962d6-ab29-5e03-b451-b28b26aec307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-4x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560566} :answer-field/id #uuid "14dacbe4-15ee-5993-8b14-d648db29810e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bd098ca-5d84-5b72-a30b-9d7d1d658d79" :question/math-academy-id "q-112097" :question/problem "![](images/2b/2bf74d1a67facef247993de2849d94918a0492acdf69b9086ac1e48a913bc6f2.png)\n\nWhich of the following polynomials could be the function shown in the diagram above?" :question/worked-solution "Let's call $P(x)$ the polynomial shown in the diagram.\n\nThe end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is positive.\n\n- the $y$-intercept is $0,$ which means that $P(0)=0.$\n\nTherefore, of the given options, the only one that satisfies all three criteria is $y=3{x}^{4}-{x}^{3}+4{x}^{2}-4x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186561060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34aa8f4e-20f1-5dda-ac53-6eb411543897" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b8241fd-ab10-5abd-9457-a6831042e1f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560391} {:answer/id #uuid "25519646-119b-5f15-ae50-e28ac0492d20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560448} {:answer/id #uuid "2c8e1779-364d-5227-a7e7-64ed9a015982" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560462} {:answer/id #uuid "65d07a26-dc02-5e5c-bb7d-8516ec1203fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560575} {:answer/id #uuid "cddaac9c-c465-53a5-b196-6c4d3033f0db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560777}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cddaac9c-c465-53a5-b196-6c4d3033f0db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560777} :answer-field/id #uuid "d22468f4-1e10-5fb7-b45d-996333ac8044" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186560954}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f2a8f457-b09c-537d-9f0d-5fb2f3f9943c" :question/math-academy-id "q-112109" :question/problem "![](images/1a/1a91d10c69c8d466b6155f775ebd0856003889089cc41a3246ec0a812b58def3.png)\n\nThe graph of the polynomial $y=P(x)$ is shown above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The degree of $P(x)$ is odd.\n\nII. $P(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nIII. The coefficient of the leading term is negative." :question/worked-solution "The end behavior of $P(x)$ is\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to -\\infty ,$ and\n\n- $P(x)\\to -\\infty $ as $x\\to \\infty .$\n\nConsequently, \n\n- the degree of $P(x)$ is even, and\n\n- the leading coefficient of $P(x)$ is negative.\n\nTherefore, statement III is true, while statements I and II are false."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186247243 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129148 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "34aa8f4e-20f1-5dda-ac53-6eb411543897" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements Regarding the Graph of a Polynomial"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6faebac8-ebda-5f28-9ae5-f47ea62ba4d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461665} {:answer/id #uuid "89f5755d-41a2-5951-8169-455f39e871ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89f5755d-41a2-5951-8169-455f39e871ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186461666} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8f6caba-9c1c-5b6e-b1e1-a94766e26a33" :answer-field/key "I" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186462586} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a776ef48-bad8-5973-b3d2-424cd31a8efb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921860